a</surname><given-names>MP</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Professions and civic engagement</article-title><source>Civic Engagement in American Democracy</source><year>1999</year><publisher-loc>Washington, DC</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Brookings Institution Press</publisher-name><fpage>163</fpage><lpage>210</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Brint S, Levy CS. Professions and civic engagement. In: Skocpol T, Fiorina MP, eds. Civic Engagement in American Democracy. Washington, DC: Brookings Institution Press; 1999:163&#x02013;210. </citation></ref><ref id="CR21"><label>21.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Krause</surname><given-names>EA</given-names></name></person-group><source>Death of the Guilds: Professions, States and the Advances of Capitalism, 1930 to the Present</source><year>1996</year><publisher-loc>New Haven, Conn</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Yale University Press</publisher-name></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Krause EA. Death of the Guilds: Professions, States and the Advances of Capitalism, 1930 to the Present. New Haven, Conn: Yale University Press; 1996. </citation></ref><ref id="CR22"><label>22.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Donini-Lenhoff</surname><given-names>FG</given-names></name><name><surname>Hedrick</surname><given-names>HL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Growth of specialization in graduate medical education</article-title><source>JAMA</source><year>2000</year><volume>284</volume><fpage>1284</fpage><lpage>1289</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1001/jama.284.10.1284</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Donini-Lenhoff FG, Hedrick HL. Growth of specialization in graduate medical education. JAMA. 2000;284:1284&#x02013;9. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10979116</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR23"><label>23.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Green</surname><given-names>LA</given-names></name><name><surname>Dodoo</surname><given-names>MS</given-names></name><name><surname>Ruddy</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><source>The Physician Workforce of the United States: a Family Medicine Perspective</source><year>2004</year><publisher-loc>Washington, DC</publisher-loc><publisher-name>American Academy of Family Physicians Robert Graham Center</publisher-name></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Green LA, Dodoo MS, Ruddy G, et al. The Physician Workforce of the United States: a Family Medicine Perspective. Washington, DC: American Academy of Family Physicians Robert Graham Center; 2004. </citation></ref><ref id="CR24"><label>24.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Arnold</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Assessing professional behavior: yesterday, today, and tomorrow</article-title><source>Acad Med</source><year>2002</year><volume>77</volume><fpage>502</fpage><lpage>515</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Arnold L. Assessing professional behavior: yesterday, today, and tomorrow. Acad Med. 2002;77:502&#x02013;15. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12063194</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR25"><label>25.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ginsburg</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Regehr</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Stern</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Lingard</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The anatomy of the professional lapse: bridging the gap between traditional frameworks and students&#x02019; perceptions</article-title><source>Acad Med</source><y